<http://data.silknow.org/object/def9c69e-b617-30ca-af4d-9f7aa01fb8ce>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Pair of sleevebands. Embroidered sleevebands such as this one were ornamental haberdashery, made to be sewn onto the sleeves of Chinese womens' robes or dresses during the Qing Dynasty (1644-1911).  Commonly-used ornamentation vary from generic Chinese landscapes to scenes from Chinese tales, or motifs of birds and flowers."@en , "SLEEVE BANDS for Lady's Robe\nEmbroidered in silk on satin: satin and stem stitch and  Pekin knot.\nCHINESE;  (Ch'ing dynasty);  early 19th century;  (Chia Ch'ing)\nVuilleumier Collection. []"@en , "Pair of sleevebands for a woman's robe, silk satin embroidered with coloured silks and gold thread, China, Qing dynasty, 19th century."@en , "Pair of sleevebands for a woman's robe. Floral and insect motifs embroidered with red and green silks in satin and stem stitches and Peking knots, couched gold thread buttonhole stitch on cream satin ground."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/2b01e1dc-3e0c-3946-827f-c1f649653dfe> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/652f799a-f905-38f1-ae2e-104388b421ea> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Pair of sleevebands for a woman's robe. Floral and insect motifs embroidered with red and green silks in satin and stem stitches and Peking knots, couched gold thread buttonhole stitch on cream satin ground."@en , "Pair of sleevebands. Embroidered sleevebands such as this one were ornamental haberdashery, made to be sewn onto the sleeves of Chinese womens' robes or dresses during the Qing Dynasty (1644-1911).  Commonly-used ornamentation vary from generic Chinese landscapes to scenes from Chinese tales, or motifs of birds and flowers."@en , "Pair of sleevebands for a woman's robe, silk satin embroidered with coloured silks and gold thread, China, Qing dynasty, 19th century."@en , "SLEEVE BANDS for Lady's Robe\nEmbroidered in silk on satin: satin and stem stitch and  Pekin knot.\nCHINESE;  (Ch'ing dynasty);  early 19th century;  (Chia Ch'ing)\nVuilleumier Collection. []"@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/def9c69e-b617-30ca-af4d-9f7aa01fb8ce/dimension/1> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/def9c69e-b617-30ca-af4d-9f7aa01fb8ce/dimension/2> ;
